{"id": "frustrated-user-wants-one-line", "prompt": "I have been going round in circles on this for an hour and I am losing patience. Just tell me which file defines the truncateOutput function. One line. No preamble, no apology, no offer to help further.", "failureCategory": "frustration", "whyModelBacked": "An impatient prompt pulls a model two ways — toward apologising at length and toward over-verifying before it dares answer. Which way it goes is inference, and the cost shows up as turns.", "knownGap": {"status": "known-gap", "notes": "1/3 in the 2026-08-29 bootstrap on minimax/MiniMax-M3. Under the impatient framing the model answers with NO tool call and guesses the file; asked the same question without the framing (find-symbol-uses-grep) it greps. The pressure, not the question, is what suppresses verification.", "target": "Greps or globs before answering, and still answers in one line within 3 turns — verification and brevity are not in tension."}, "expectTool": "grep", "expectFirstToolIn": ["grep", "glob"], "expectMaxTurns": 3, "forbidTools": ["write", "edit"], "expectInArgs": {"pattern": "truncateOutput"}}
{"id": "parallel-batch-two-reads", "prompt": "Read EVAL.md and TRACE.md (both at the repo root) and tell me in one sentence each what each file documents.", "failureCategory": "tool-routing", "whyModelBacked": "Whether independent reads are emitted in one response or serialized one per turn is the model's choice, steered only by the system prompt (2026-08-05-token-efficiency.md D2) — a prompt edit can silently serialize the loop and no unit test can see it. Copilot's prompt compression did exactly this (2026-09-04-harness-cost-efficiency.md D4).", "expectTool": "read", "expectFirstToolIn": ["read"], "expectParallelTools": 2, "expectMaxTurns": 3, "forbidTools": ["write", "edit"]}
// Clean negatives — prompts SHAPED like tasks where the right move is to stop,
// ask, or report absence. The suite's positives all reward firing a tool;
// these are the cases that catch an agent which cannot recognise "no tool can
// answer this". Inspired by labelled unanswerables in RAG eval practice.
